#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "ac2a2137-53fa-5121-b541-02e0addd8757" :question/math-academy-id "e-12356" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}$, where\n\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \\quad \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!, \\quad \\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}$.\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖) \\\\\n&= (⟨z,-y,-x⟩)/(\\sqrt{(z)^{2} + (-y)^{2} + (-x)^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{z^{2} + y^{2} + x^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨x, 2z, z - y⟩ \\cdot \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨x, 2z, z - y⟩ \\cdot ⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (xz - 2yz - x(z - y))/(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{xy - 2yz}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{x y - 2 y z}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "fba5ed01-4c4c-50f6-8060-76bf40df79b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12384" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$." :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x\\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0\\leq y\\leq 1\n$$\n\nthe downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨z, z, 4xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1, 4xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&=-4xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)(-4xy)dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-4xy)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(-4xy)dxdy \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{1}2ydy∫_{0}^{1}2xdx \\\\\n&= - [y^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [x^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "35c3c9ba-60b0-5bcf-8e9c-2862ece44e7e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12454" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$.\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r_x' \\times \\mathbf r_y' \\mid \\mid =3$.*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface\n\n$$\nz = 2x+2y\n$$\n\ncan be written using the dot product as\n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S$, we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖) \\\\\n&= (⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩)/(\\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 1^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nz=2x+2y\n$$\n\non $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨y, 0, z⟩ \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨y, 0, 2x + 2y⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(-2y + 0x + 2x + 2y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{2}{3}xdS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot \\mid \\mid r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} \\mid \\mid dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot 3dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot 3dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2xdA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}2xdxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}dy \\cdot ∫_{0}^{2}2xdx \\\\\n&= [y]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [x^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f632e1-bc4a-5e20-9d42-993ea0ac140f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + 2yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163803} {:answer/id #uuid "d9597573-13cf-5b29-80fc-a0cec25c3d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163804} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf7721d-433b-562f-8b02-6dc0eaf18ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy - yz + 2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163805} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a59231-39be-5bd9-b000-7fd5bea7b50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy + yz + xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163806} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfc1e63-d79d-5c0a-a85e-bb0f5321e73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + 3yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9597573-13cf-5b29-80fc-a0cec25c3d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163804} :answer-field/id #uuid "800fbb75-73ff-5c83-b570-3144244101d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163802}] :question/id #uuid "7492e5fe-6cc9-5d4b-8d1f-20d212147cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $n^(~)$, where\n\n$F(x, y, z) = ⟨z, y, x⟩, n^(~)(x, y, z) = ⟨2y,-z, 2x⟩, n = (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖)$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(S)\\frac{AAAAAAA}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?"}]}
